<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayeux Tapestry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1070s</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Tapestry is an embroidered cloth about 70 metres long depicting the events leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman conquest of England</a>, ending with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066. It was probably made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the 1070s for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Odo of Bayeux and long hung in Bayeux Cathedral. Among its scenes is Count Guy of Ponthieu's capture of the shipwrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry.jpg" alt="Bayeux Tapestry" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odo_bayeux_tapestry.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known written reference to the tapestry is a 1476 inventory of Bayeux Cathedral, and its origins have been much debated. French legend credited Queen Matilda, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, and her ladies. Scholars of the 20th century concluded that it was probably commissioned by William's half-brother Bishop Odo of Bayeux, who became Earl of Kent after the Conquest. Three of Odo's followers named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> appear on it, Odo himself appears four times, and it was kept in the cathedral he built, possibly made for its dedication in 1077.</p><p>Most scholars argue that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> artists made it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, probably in or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, since its style is close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the monastic libraries there, Odo's English base was in Kent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text shows hints of Old English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work is an embroidery rather than a true tapestry, worked in wool on a linen ground about 68 metres long and 70 centimetres tall, with ten natural dye colours. Nine linen panels were embroidered separately and sewn together. The surviving cloth has 58 scenes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> captions, and the final scenes are missing.</p><p>The story begins in 1064 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> to Normandy. Harold is captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu">Guy I of Ponthieu</a>, released to William, joins his campaign against Conan II of Brittany and swears an oath on relics, though the tapestry does not say what he promised. It ends with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066, and nearly half of its scenes precede the invasion. Whether the figure struck by an arrow is Harold is disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The cloth was hung each year in Bayeux Cathedral for the week of the feast of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>. It survived the sack of Bayeux by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in 1562 and narrowly escaped destruction in the French Revolution, and scholars rediscovered it in 1729. Because it was probably commissioned by the House of Normandy and shows a Norman viewpoint, many modern scholars regard it as a justification of the Conquest, although Harold is shown as brave. It is a source on 11th-century clothing, castles and ships, and the only surviving example of medieval narrative needlework.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayeux Tapestry” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy also produced a rich historical literature, from Dudo of Saint-Quentin and William of Jumièges to Orderic Vitalis, while the <b>Bayeux Tapestry</b>, probably made in England in the 1070s, tells the story of the conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It became a bishopric, and in the 11th century the <b>Bayeux Tapestry</b> was made for its cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/state/baiocasses">Baiocasses</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1064 Count Guy I captured the shipwrecked Harold Godwinson and handed him to William of Normandy, as shown on the <b>Bayeux Tapestry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ponthieu">County of Ponthieu</a></li></ul></section>
